*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.config text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# This file last. With core.autocrlf=true and no rule of its own, a Windows checkout leaves
# it CRLF - measured at 65 CR after the decompiled/ rule below was added, one per line. Git
# tolerates CRLF in an attribute file, so nothing was broken; but the one file that decides
# every other file's line endings was the one file no rule covered.
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Files under the overlay that are shell scripts but have no .sh suffix. 02_network ends in a
# line continuation (`ucidef_set_interface ... \`), so a CRLF checkout makes the device run
# "... <CR>" as an argument and the whole lan definition is lost - on a board whose only
# reachable port is eth0, that is an image with no address at all.
overlays/**/etc/board.d/* text eol=lf
overlays/**/lib/upgrade/platform.sh text eol=lf
overlays/**/etc/rc.d/* text eol=lf

# kernel-overlays/ is copied straight into the kernel tree. core.autocrlf=true is the
# default on a Windows checkout, and without this rule git rewrites every file here to
# CRLF on the way out - which is how zx279133-sk-d840n.dts arrived with 432 CR bytes in a
# working tree whose committed blob has zero. The gates in build-sk-d840n.yml match on
# line shapes (`^/ \{`, `status = "okay"`), so a stray CR is not cosmetic: it is a gate
# reading text the build will never see. The committed blobs were always LF - this pins
# the working tree to the same thing so an offline check means what it says.
kernel-overlays/** text eol=lf

# The PC-side tools/, docs/ and pon/ trees, brought under version control so the fix for
# gettrial.sh (two defects) and preflight.sh (one) stop being local-only. Scoped to these
# directories instead of a broad *.py / *.txt / *.c rule so the intent stays readable: a
# future file outside these three trees has to argue for its own EOL policy.
#
# An earlier revision of this comment said a broad rule plus a renormalize pass "would
# rewrite the already-committed decompiled/*.fdtdump.txt blobs, and that is a different
# question". Measured, that was wrong: those blobs were already LF, so no rule rewrites
# them. decompiled/ now carries its own rule at the bottom of this file.
#
# pon/*.txt is the one place where this could have destroyed data rather than cosmetics:
# those are raw captures of ioctl numbers, proc output and netlink monitors, so a CR in
# them might have been a captured byte instead of a line ending. Measured first - in all
# five files every CR is followed by an LF and the files that mix CRLF and LF terminators
# still have zero lone CRs - so normalising loses nothing. A file where the CR did not
# terminate a line would have shown a lone CR and this rule would not be safe.
tools/** text eol=lf
docs/** text eol=lf
pon/** text eol=lf

# The vendor .ko files under pon/vendor-modules/ are excluded by .gitignore and must stay
# that way. They are proprietary ZTE/Skyworth objects, not text: -text makes a forced
# `git add -f` store them verbatim instead of running them through EOL normalisation.
pon/vendor-modules/** -text

# decompiled/ has exactly one producer: .github/workflows/inspect-dtb.yml runs
# `dtc -f -I dtb -O dts` and `fdtdump` on ubuntu-24.04 and commits the result with
# `git add -f decompiled`, so every blob already arrives as LF. No workflow reads the
# committed copies back - the one grep inspect-dtb.yml does runs against the file it just
# wrote, in the same job - so the CRLF these four files picked up was never reaching a gate.
#
# With no rule here and core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default), a checkout rewrote all
# four to CRLF while the committed blobs stayed at zero CR. Measured before this rule was
# written: working tree CR = 2373 / 2115 / 2433 / 2163, every blob CR = 0.
#
# lone-CR = 0 is the part that makes normalising safe rather than lossy: in all four blobs
# every CR terminates a line, so there is no captured byte to destroy. Same test that let
# pon/*.txt be normalised above. Without it this rule would have been a guess.
#
# The renormalise pass this rule accompanies is a no-op by construction - the blobs are
# already LF, so their object ids do not move. It only makes the working tree agree with
# what is already committed.
decompiled/** text eol=lf

# The paths a Windows cloner was still getting wrong after the rules above. Measured on a
# fresh clone of this repository with core.autocrlf=true: nine files arrived carrying CR that
# their committed blob does not contain. Seven of those are text whose blob is pure LF with
# zero lone CR, so normalising them loses nothing - the same test pon/*.txt passed.
#
# Three of the seven are shell scripts - trial-files/etc/rc.d/S15trial,
# trial-files/lib/preinit/05_trial_alive and trial-files/usr/libexec/trial-blink - and
# tools/mktrialram.py patches exactly this tree into the device initramfs. A CR there is not
# whitespace to a shell: it becomes part of the command the board runs. That is the same
# failure mode already documented for overlays/**/etc/rc.d/* and 02_network, arriving through
# a door nobody had checked.
#
# overlays/** and trial-files/** are written directory-wide because a NUL-byte scan of all
# 128 tracked files found exactly two binaries and neither is under either tree. Re-check
# that before widening a rule further.
.gitignore text eol=lf
overlays/** text eol=lf
trial-files/** text eol=lf

# tests/ is the rehearsal suite: 39 suites plus the helpers they exec. The blanket *.sh rule
# above already covers its 46 shell files; the Python ones need a rule of their own, because no
# broad *.py rule exists here and one was not added on purpose - a new tree argues for its own
# policy rather than inheriting a guess.
#
# This is not cosmetic for this directory in particular. regress_round_backup.sh sweeps
# staging/diag-20261014/*.sh for CR and fails the whole run on any hit, so on a core.autocrlf
# Windows checkout the suite would fail on its own hygiene check instead of on the behaviour it
# exists to check - a failure that teaches the reader to ignore red.
# Measured on the delivered copy before committing: 104 files, CR total = 0.
tests/** text eol=lf

# The only two binaries in the repository. Each blob holds ~26k NUL bytes and 86 CR bytes,
# and a fresh clone reproduces the same 86, so autocrlf has not damaged them and there is no
# bug to fix here. -text is a lock, not a repair: it stops a future renormalise pass or a
# forced `git add -f` from reading those CR bytes as line endings and rewriting a device tree.
dtb/** -text
